[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367d9d83-fa45-5cce-9d65-a6d78ae2576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324417} {:answer/id #uuid "4c724626-9b94-5e62-973b-cd52608f720a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324600} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6afcfc-2d28-59af-a54f-d23a7c8ccc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325039} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b18ed0-0130-54fa-a1a2-cf0a61fccd5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{85}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325350} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c96eb8-7035-5f26-b66a-0a1d156defa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{155}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2b18ed0-0130-54fa-a1a2-cf0a61fccd5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{85}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325350} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d956283-05d2-55de-82bf-25c1a5cc7f97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c002c85-6997-51d7-b78d-ab1c68bac4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39020" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=4xy.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $21$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{8}}{6}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{85}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{85}{2})}{21}=\\frac{85}{42}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118c0a6d-2d86-5402-ab17-e9a5a70e791e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481820} {:answer/id #uuid "1a807e54-a4b6-5ff5-bbcc-a7cd1282fba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481845} {:answer/id #uuid "8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482107} {:answer/id #uuid "97ba92a3-dcbc-53fc-b77a-c4ddb79e17c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482131} {:answer/id #uuid "feda8ec9-58b4-5dda-999b-6d6a16d95a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482107} :answer-field/id #uuid "92bb2c37-88bf-542c-b72c-3da1ea32bcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d27a7ac-3737-5071-b997-af83c998bd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39054" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le 2x+y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef7332e4-b981-54af-8da2-fcdb494e9902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197655} {:answer/id #uuid "281c6df6-036f-5860-be8c-11e9d432022a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197656} {:answer/id #uuid "7557b355-b5e6-5c35-9ec5-ce7baaeba82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197658} {:answer/id #uuid "55d05532-6ff9-54f2-9d4c-bf3ea90714ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481979} {:answer/id #uuid "828f75f9-fca6-5a07-93ba-87dbb17ec87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef7332e4-b981-54af-8da2-fcdb494e9902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197655} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb66677-2e9a-5652-8302-63d8739cb7ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3982f9b1-ca56-5e4b-8f97-4f23fb6f2fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39055" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+z\\le 2,\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881b0dd0-01c6-5013-9685-0e58c6da71c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197661} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a11a8a-6503-5229-93d1-630340faec00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197662} {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} {:answer/id #uuid "3404d280-8cad-5644-8ec1-3e6e08824288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197664} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd7243b-48c8-50fa-aaa2-6aa1ef439e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd3b3e1-98ae-532b-94b3-c5b9b8ab641b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b45928b-ff82-5faf-a3d8-f16998390abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39060" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "828ef39e-8044-56b7-ae7e-31b3a2d4b1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325070} {:answer/id #uuid "a65f4ed3-b1ed-595c-b75f-c3d4bae8b528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325388} {:answer/id #uuid "b6886231-0700-522c-b955-3f6e44128cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325538} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bfdad9-5057-5e0b-bb84-a06071db39cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325830} {:answer/id #uuid "ebce7bc8-de4e-52b2-88e5-f11d3f195b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6886231-0700-522c-b955-3f6e44128cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325538} :answer-field/id #uuid "768d0c8f-75e7-5c0c-bdf7-17312b46abcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b2d988e7-dd3f-5642-ad43-14d09def5c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-39415" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b7/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/60/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f7a953-e9af-5467-ab0c-28f1410db92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373020} {:answer/id #uuid "432f6fab-3964-529a-bbf0-a238352cf6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡zx}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373021} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8ccfe7-1523-5162-a963-66f1b92a1814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}^{\\pi /4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373022} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c374c7-d886-5f5e-844f-9167b678a4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373023} {:answer/id #uuid "7591aef0-12aa-5113-bf39-1d91ab245bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡z}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8ccfe7-1523-5162-a963-66f1b92a1814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}^{\\pi /4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373022} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1dafd5-d127-587c-b3bf-f67c1594cf6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db074f68-4935-5a60-b671-6e46f6804cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-39418" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z.$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32a019900b77c0bb84b4599b8899e3d0d32ae07d9674da126db34394b97ea24.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{tan}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/af/af629672ff2fd31eeae585ad4941fb8682941444cd34383392fb0a6bec24396e.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04dc917237ab002263e35fb4a84c264fef240fefaa456c5249d6e24d0c8ebe28.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b2a6fc02441bb6013b6f54d49ceefafc1468b291ae4149847290135bbf2d2e56.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\n![](images/b9/b942dbfba8276421620df6258c9951c2b91a5d2dea18c84260c90b6cbe6f760b.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}^{\\pi /4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c606e01-9dfb-5365-baff-6cf96dfe091b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324327} {:answer/id #uuid "49ac0c42-1f92-5a7b-bad2-5d73a9bc784e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324571} {:answer/id #uuid "57465dee-acfc-58dc-90be-047f0c45dfbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324695} {:answer/id #uuid "869bb5a9-2548-5ebd-a947-73bfe056042f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325116} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93d683-6e91-578e-a28e-af59ff21dc95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa93d683-6e91-578e-a28e-af59ff21dc95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325423} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb1536c-7770-5a45-b769-e2ba65d91de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38ead2a6-fa96-5840-9429-dd70fd1bb582" :question/math-academy-id "q-39547" :question/problem "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}$ and is enclosed between the curve $x=1-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{15}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/78/78f9da3d48727d2cb3c447be0f4050a044c4aabe29179fd31f04cd7b6c66eabd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot (1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{105})}{(\\frac{4}{15})}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272de190-0e96-5d51-95d4-ff366ae72e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348785} {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} {:answer/id #uuid "93ea44ae-a889-5898-8990-fdfc9599ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349770} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdca57-4e7f-505d-ba98-23b6018b411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350265} {:answer/id #uuid "f22a24d4-0975-5c9c-9994-b66abef9c47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6831b19c-e658-54e3-b4bb-f0560cf60cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf91dd3-a758-5a4f-91d7-508b8452bd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197917} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} {:answer/id #uuid "50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480887} {:answer/id #uuid "69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480936} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ff9f93-9496-5f90-8fb6-64a192e2c4d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7817742-b76a-5484-9fbf-d87d76ee15f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203672 :question/id #uuid "df4bd6dd-74c1-5681-b949-b324aaf535ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-13354" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n> $P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n> $\\lnot P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{5}$ is rational\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is rational.\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> Then,\n> $\\sqrt[3]{5} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to cube both sides of the equation to find a factor of $a$ and a factor of $b$.\n\n> This implies that\n> $\\sqrt[3]{5}|= \\frac{a}{b}; (\\sqrt[3]{5})^{3}|= (\\frac{a}{b})^{3}; 5|= \\frac{a^{3}}{b^{3}}; 5b^{3}|= a^{3}$,\n> which means that $5 \\mid a^3$.\n\nNow, recall the following property of divisibility by a prime $p{:}$\n$p \\mid xyz \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (p \\mid x) \\lor (p \\mid y) \\lor (p \\mid z)$\nApplying this property with $p = 5$ and\n\n$$\nx = y = z = a\n$$\n\nwe have\n$5 \\mid a^3 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5 \\mid a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (5 \\mid a) \\lor (5 \\mid a) \\lor (5 \\mid a)$.\nSo, we have that $5 \\mid a$.\n\n> Since $5$ is prime, we have $5 \\mid a$, which means $a = 5c$ where $c$ is an integer.\n\nNow, we substitute $a = 5c$ into the equation $5b^3 = a^3$ and show that $5$ is also a factor of $b$.\n\n> Therefore, we have that\n> $5b^{3}|= (5c)^{3}; 5b^{3}|= 125c^{3}; b^{3}|= 25c^{3}; b^{3}|= 5 \\cdot 5c^{3}$.\n> Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $5 \\mid b^3$, which means that $5 \\mid b$.\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $5$, their greatest common divisor is at least $5$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> Combining the results $5 \\mid a$ and $5 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 5$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203723 :question/id #uuid "54bfc9d4-2a5a-595c-b20e-51c8c42b8cd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14967" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that*\n$p \\mid a^n \\Rightarrow p \\mid a$\n*for any prime $p$.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n> $P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n> $\\lnot P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{15}$ is rational\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is rational.\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> Then,\n> $\\sqrt[3]{15} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to cube both sides of the equation to find a factor of $a$ and a factor of $b$.\n\n> This implies that\n> $\\sqrt[3]{15}|= \\frac{a}{b}; (\\sqrt[3]{15})^{3}|= (\\frac{a}{b})^{3}; 15|= \\frac{a^{3}}{b^{3}}; 15b^{3}|= a^{3}$,\n> which means that $15 \\mid a^3$.\n\nWe need to show that $15\\mid a$.\n\n> Let's use this to show that $15\\mid a$.\n\nTo do this, recall the following divisibility properties:\n\n- Transitivity of divisibility:\n$(x\\mid y) \\land (y\\mid z) \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x\\mid z$\n- Divisibility of a power by a prime:\n$p \\mid a^n \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad p \\mid a$\n- Divisibility by a product:\n$(x\\mid z)\\land(y\\mid z)\\land(\\textrm{gcd}(x,y) = 1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad xy\\mid z$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n15=3\\cdot 5\n$$\n\nis the product of two primes, which suggests we may want to use the third property.\n\nAlso, if $15$ divides $a^3$, then $3$ and $5$ must divide $a^3$ by the first property.\n\nWe start by using the first property:\n\n> First, note that $15 = 3\\cdot 5$. By the transitivity of divisibility,\n> $(3∣15)∧(15∣a^{3})⇒3∣a^{3}$.\n\nNow, we use the second property:\n\n> Since $3$ is prime, this means that $3 \\mid a$.\n\nThe argument to show that $5\\mid a$ is identical, so we simply state that.\n\n> We also have $5 \\mid a$ by a similar argument.\n\nSince $3$ and $5$ are coprime, we can show that $15\\mid a$ by the third property.\n\n> Since $3\\mid a$ and $5\\mid a$, and $\\textrm{gcd(3,5)} =1$, we have that $15\\mid a$ which means that $a=15c$ for some integer $c$.\n\nNow, we substitute $a=15c$ into the equation $15b^3 = a^3$ and show that $15$ is also a factor of $b$.\n\n> Therefore, we have that\n> $15b^{3}|= (15c)^{3}; 15b^{3}|= 3, 375c^{3}; b^{3}|= 225c^{3}$.\n\nThen, we use the first property again:\n\n> Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $225 \\mid b^3$. By the transitivity of divisibility,\n> $(15∣225)∧(225∣b^{3})⇒15∣b^{3}$,\n> which means that $15 \\mid b$.\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $15$, their greatest common divisor is at least $15$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> Combining the results $15 \\mid a$ and $15 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 15$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98386397-3c55-556b-982d-544f8634155b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = 3\\sin (\\frac{πt}{5}) + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199693} {:answer/id #uuid "f62e3ee6-8144-500e-a86f-85048b1d7d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = 5\\sin (\\frac{πt}{12}) + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199694} {:answer/id #uuid "02dfdbfa-ccc0-5495-a1f9-426c23cf0500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = 3\\sin (\\frac{πt}{6}) + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199695} {:answer/id #uuid "6e49c6b3-0166-5e0a-a707-ef0ffad68541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = 3\\sin (\\frac{πt}{12}) + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199696} {:answer/id #uuid "7af95703-3978-5021-8404-9c4e189ba17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = 5\\sin (\\frac{πt}{6}) + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199697}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f36b5775-f68e-5080-8d31-0b8f70e97075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199692}] :question/id #uuid "37580f76-fc6e-58a2-a0e1-1ad38c2d259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-136147" :question/problem "The daily tide level in a coastal city, measured in meters, can be modeled by a function of the form $H(t) = A\\sin (Bt) + C$, where $A$, $B$, $C$ are constants and $t$ is the time (in hours) after midnight.\n\nIt is observed that at midnight, when the tide is rising, its level is exactly between the lowest and highest tides. The lowest possible tide level is $2m$, and the tide reaches its highest level of $8m$ at $3a.m$.\n\nWhich of the following could be the function $H(t)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfafe656-3287-50c2-a2c5-912c0e462a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) =-15\\cos (\\frac{πt}{20}) + 17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199699} {:answer/id #uuid "06fdce6c-f55e-518c-9873-d581261fddba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = \\cos (\\frac{πt}{40}) + 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199700} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1c93df-86de-5988-a9bd-7306ec0df337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) =-17\\cos (\\frac{πt}{40}) + 17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199701} {:answer/id #uuid "621e8bd7-1cd6-56a7-bafc-5ea9c8884906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) =-10\\cos (\\frac{πt}{20}) + 32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199702} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4213f6-40ef-5114-aaaa-0614d6fc4fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(t) = 15\\cos (\\frac{πt}{20}) + 32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199703}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ad370ce3-4459-544d-a729-cc105c6bfc44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199698}] :question/id #uuid "3d4b8df3-23c6-5d50-ac56-87cd00f8e80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-136536" :question/problem "The height above the ground of a seat on a Ferris wheel, in feet, is modeled by a function of the form $H(t) = A\\cos (Bt) + C$, where $A$, $B$, $C$ are constants, and $t$ is the time (in seconds) since the wheel started the ride.\n\nIt is observed that when $t = 0$, the seat is $2$ feet off the ground, the lowest height it can reach. The maximum height of $32$ feet is reached $20$ seconds after the wheel has started to rotate.\n\nWhich of the following could be the function $H(t)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999a5ab2-f8f0-5fd9-9601-100407e06720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{π}\\arctan (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199786} {:answer/id #uuid "bed59554-b0e8-5845-8251-555802d95690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{2x}{π})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199787} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbd92c5-9403-5510-9a57-b86d204a535d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}\\arctan (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199788} {:answer/id #uuid "5d295d16-c6e5-568c-93c9-99e16981d7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{πx}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199789}] :answer-field/id #uuid "15830b2f-a71b-5882-bc26-394b80e2bb17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a064a41f-f258-5f50-b64f-04113d2e8923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199791} {:answer/id #uuid "b0986620-303a-532c-9572-87c9a455e817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199792} {:answer/id #uuid "4eef62ad-addb-5aa1-b0b6-e9e65b9894cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199793}] :answer-field/id #uuid "25ebced9-3b9c-5c75-9adb-9b8db41fde55" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac1e5f0a-ee39-5ca8-a90c-06c1bab76c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\ne & B\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199795} {:answer/id #uuid "6aedda3b-47b1-508c-b3c8-cbbbb32e943c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & B\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199796}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aa31923a-3ee9-5a17-bbe0-c74f480cba25" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dadac7be-a12c-5b3a-9b72-7a6b8d4217fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199798} {:answer/id #uuid "235cf3d2-6348-5943-bae7-0979679f6c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199799} {:answer/id #uuid "6b96f80f-fe03-5257-b5d1-b2490c6da0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "injective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199800}] :answer-field/id #uuid "063a6ac2-770a-53c7-8327-aa79dc8fb14e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199797}] :question/id #uuid "068fea0a-29fc-59d8-90a8-c9d7db39e6cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-181622" :question/problem "$A = (-1, 1), B = R$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: B → A$, defined as\n$\\frac{π}{2}\\arctan (x)$ {{field-1}}\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d102bdbc-617d-5cc0-a1f6-816fc8646b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V(t) = 35\\cos (\\frac{2πt}{3}) + 105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200744} {:answer/id #uuid "d7664bb6-71ca-5eca-b5e6-bcbfb1dc2ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V(t) = 35\\cos (\\frac{2πt}{5}) + 105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200745} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f65ab8-b103-5e5f-b00b-c6d008f40090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V(t) = 35\\cos (2πt) + 140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200746} {:answer/id #uuid "47e75aed-129d-56e4-ac2a-d01ec9a2e09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V(t) = 35\\cos (\\frac{2πt}{5}) + 70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200747} {:answer/id #uuid "55b5377c-6b97-524d-bb5d-594b7eea9993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V(t) = 35\\cos (\\frac{2πt}{3}) + 70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200748}] :answer-field/id #uuid "47d9f5b8-20a9-5657-bd53-c9364cfbcb25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200743}] :question/id #uuid "702e797c-2a31-5bca-b448-9210d66bb91d" :question/math-academy-id "q-223212" :question/problem "A heart pumps blood in and out of its chambers. The volume of blood in the heart, in milliliters, can be modeled by a function of the form $V(t) = 35\\cos (Bt) + C$, where $B$ and $C$ are constants and $t$ is the time (in seconds).\n\nIt is observed that when $t = 0$, the heart contains its maximum volume of blood, $140$ milliliters. Then, after $1.5$ seconds, the heart contains its minimum volume of blood.\n\nWhich of the following could be the function $V(t)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208272 :question/id #uuid "fce0099e-b010-50a4-bf0d-51aca4380129" :question/math-academy-id "e-17865" :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$ where \n![](images/38/38fae06690f00ec57f1c56f5b3c0f4add88d5505a991f7cbd09020364c973238.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} L(\\mathbf{x})$ at ![](images/62/6267a6f3c8df9f11cfa395a92d426ab1901e9048cadc6b50640fc5aafc0b59df.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}.$ Then, $L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y},$ and\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} L = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}L}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}\\right)^T = \n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}\\right)^T \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}L}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}\\right)^T.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}\\right) = \\mathbf{y}^T \n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}) = \\mathbf{A},\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/41/41633d52787dbf98d039bf414f07a7052fe0acf0b55c831bbaf24868b22e95df.svg), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 81 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208392 :question/id #uuid "7c5708bc-95e0-5671-845c-9fa02bfdc20a" :question/math-academy-id "e-19995" :question/problem "What is the sum of the solutions to the equation given below?\n$\\left| \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6} \\right| = \\dfrac{7}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}$, then\n$\\mid \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6} \\mid |= \\frac{7}{3}; \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6}|= \\frac{7}{3}; \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{7}{3} + \\frac{11}{6}; \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{14}{6} + \\frac{11}{6}; \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{25}{6}; x|= \\frac{25}{6} \\cdot \\frac{5}{3}; x|= \\frac{125}{18}$.\n- If $\\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right)$, then\n$\\mid \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6} \\mid |= \\frac{7}{3}; - (\\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6})|= \\frac{7}{3}; - \\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{11}{6}|= \\frac{7}{3}; - \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{7}{3} - \\frac{11}{6}; - \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{14}{6} - \\frac{11}{6}; - \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{3}{6}; x|= \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3}); x|=-\\frac{15}{18}; x|=-\\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{125}{18} + \\left(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{125}{18} - \\dfrac{15}{18} = \\dfrac{110}{18} = \\dfrac{55}{9}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208440 :question/id #uuid "ca0e78d2-4a22-5bbf-a92f-a877a2472f35" :question/math-academy-id "e-20407" :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 y = 6 x + 5 \\\\ - 4 y = 2 x - 11\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $40x$?" :question/worked-solution "We will solve the system using elimination.\n\nSince the left-hand sides are $4y$ and $-4y$, we can add the equations to eliminate $y{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\cancel{4 y} & \\displaystyle  = 6 x + 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{- 4 y} & \\displaystyle  = 2 x - 11 \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle  = ( 6 x + 2 x ) + ( 5 - 11 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle  = 8 x - 6 \\\\ \\displaystyle 8 x & \\displaystyle  = 6 \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{8}} \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $40x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40x &= 40(\\frac{3}{4}) = 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208447 :question/id #uuid "782b055b-cbde-5496-9b2c-3bded723512c" :question/math-academy-id "e-20447" :question/problem "In $\\triangle R S T$, the measure of $\\angle R$ is $58^{\\circ}$, the measure of $\\angle S$ is $90^{\\circ}$, and the measure of $\\angle T$ is $\\left(\\dfrac{z}{3}\\right)^{\\circ}$. What is the value of $z$?" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$. For the given $\\triangle RST$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠R + m∠S + m∠T &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n58^{∘} + 90^{∘} + (\\frac{z}{3})^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n148^{∘} + (\\frac{z}{3})^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n(\\frac{z}{3})^{∘} &= 32^{∘} \\\\\n\\frac{z}{3} &= 32 \\\\\nz &= 96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $z$ is $96$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208520 :question/id #uuid "62f09140-f9b7-5d48-b357-d8fe981e2c5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-20822" :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{5}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{10}} \\\\ a x + b y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{10}}\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $a$ and $b$ are constants. The system has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$?" :question/worked-solution "A system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line. This occurs when both equations have the same slope and the same $y$-intercept.\n\nIn other words, the two equations represent the same line if one equation is a constant multiple of the other.\n\nWe start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier.\n\nWe multiply both sides of the first equation by $10$ (the least common multiple of $5$, $2$, and $10$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10(-\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{1}{2}y) &= 10(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\\n- 6x + 5y &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also multiply both sides of the second equation by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10(ax + by) &= 10(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\\n10ax + 10by &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system becomes\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 6 x + 5 y = 7 \\\\ 10 a x + 10 b y = 7\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all coefficients and constants are proportional. That means\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10a}{-6} = \\dfrac{10b}{5} = \\dfrac{7}{7}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}\n$$\n\nwe focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10a}{-6} = \\dfrac{10b}{5}\n$$\n\nIt gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 10 a \\cdot  5 & \\displaystyle  = 10 b \\cdot  ( - 6 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 50 a & \\displaystyle  = - 60 b \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{60}{50}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}} & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{- {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{5}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c3cde9-216b-5b06-a2d7-f17bcc359a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531937} {:answer/id #uuid "952049ad-9e8a-5b7c-8a40-1b529b790b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532273} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e551dd-aff9-5cdf-a21e-dee3bfc6a1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532366} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d16db9-d28a-5208-8418-ea2e4224aad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 32 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532414} {:answer/id #uuid "eedfdf30-92c3-5e54-aaf9-5fb07ffd1c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 32 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eedfdf30-92c3-5e54-aaf9-5fb07ffd1c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 32 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532449} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ac8dc5d-b24d-5234-9c6b-196229d7d8cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa5725de-7db0-574b-a1ac-d3be9e0fac87" :question/math-academy-id "q-269389" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 32 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0288d1c8-90da-55c4-98e8-5a5eabefe8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539481} {:answer/id #uuid "86d96929-a573-5ba1-a568-15337e2bcf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539703} {:answer/id #uuid "cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539828} {:answer/id #uuid "e136544d-ec3c-563b-b13e-653feae14454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539864} {:answer/id #uuid "ff114911-6bea-5d83-a4a0-4820ac877a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539828} :answer-field/id #uuid "6839df18-40b0-55d8-b0ee-244e7c3bdebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b2d453b-0edb-52e1-a8ad-599845d2374a" :question/math-academy-id "q-269515" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)={y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b32acde0-321c-51bb-b77b-7a3379edaf18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205718}] :question/id #uuid "201019d8-d3c7-5638-9791-f35c92f31a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-283705" :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = x + \\frac{5}{7}$. What is the value of $g$ when $x = \\frac{2}{7}$?$g(\\frac{2}{7}) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "7f6c519a-66af-50cf-9a33-2e7e289f3ce0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186206016}] :question/id #uuid "7eacb426-8e9d-5821-abc7-1009533613dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-285566" :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 2(\\frac{1}{3} - x)^{2} + \\frac{5}{3}$. What is the value of $g(\\frac{1}{3})$?$g(\\frac{1}{3}) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ea7b36-b14d-5422-ae77-0765f4beaebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207774} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5ac0d1-a72a-57fd-8d2d-c63de56fd6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207775} {:answer/id #uuid "58d99c13-753d-5c76-98c2-c00c360ff906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207776} {:answer/id #uuid "5e235900-bd4a-571a-b5f7-881d78665c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207777} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac1788-804b-5178-8b9d-c021e963eefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207778}] :answer-field/id #uuid "04f138e5-a718-5046-bead-4a815cc1aec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207773}] :question/id #uuid "fc7273d9-caa6-5433-b44f-7627429937ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-307028" :question/problem "In $△ABC$, the measure of $∠A$ is $52^{∘}$, the measure of $∠B$ is $90^{∘}$, and the measure of $∠C$ is $(\\frac{z}{4})^{∘}$. What is the value of $z$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ceb4d6e-2096-57e5-b18b-e7886f43622d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207780} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d6d714-f027-5538-b8e1-a713a4a51675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207781} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dcee68-9007-519c-9a9c-4531babdd57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207782} {:answer/id #uuid "2331cdc5-d6bb-5276-8f41-65d26a3ef37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207783} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba8cd52-a8ad-5107-8249-cbc7de8d5c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207784}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d960d9-e15d-5dc6-a39f-1bfdfcf81aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186207779}] :question/id #uuid "b8ccf9c7-7f22-5c7a-984d-0fa451363956" :question/math-academy-id "q-307037" :question/problem "In $△RST$, the measure of $∠R$ is $58^{∘}$, the measure of $∠S$ is $90^{∘}$, and the measure of $∠T$ is $(\\frac{z}{3})^{∘}$. What is the value of $z$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212820 :question/id #uuid "33cc9ad9-0ba8-5f65-adb6-41b05eccfc3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-18987" :question/problem "Factor the expression $y^2-\\dfrac{1}{10}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n10 = \\big(\\sqrt{10}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2} - \\frac{1}{10} &= y^{2} - (1)/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) \\\\ &= y^{2} - (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})^{2} = (y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212849 :question/id #uuid "11d3b3ea-12ee-5d4a-b66b-0f312638e31f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19023" :question/problem "Factor the expression $\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36$." :question/worked-solution "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2} - 3^{2} \\\\ &= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3)(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212859 :question/id #uuid "9707f903-49f4-592f-a266-a7837f7f5542" :question/math-academy-id "e-19035" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212860 :question/id #uuid "ed802710-305b-5ee1-a5c4-02808862e7af" :question/math-academy-id "e-19036" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t$ as a power of $\\dfrac43$ only." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{4}{3})^{3}(\\frac{4}{3})^{t} &= (\\frac{4}{3})^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212872 :question/id #uuid "e1521bf1-21d2-513a-842b-d2187c64e34d" :question/math-academy-id "e-19053" :question/problem "The function\n$C(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}$\nmodels the number of data packets processed, in thousands, by a server $t$ minutes after a throughput upgrade. According to the model, in how many seconds is the number of packets expected to increase by $5\\%$?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nC(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the number of data packets processed, in thousands, $t$ minutes after the upgrade. We want to rewrite this model in terms of seconds rather than minutes.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{s}{60}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $60$ (not multiply) because we want $s=60$ seconds to correspond to $t=1$ minute.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $c(s)$ for the number of packets as a function of time in seconds $s$ after the upgrade:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(s) = C(\\frac{s}{60}) &= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2) \\cdot (s/60)} \\\\\n&= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{s/24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe base $1.05$ means that the number of packets grows by $5\\%$ each time the exponent increases by $1$, i.e., once every growth period.\n\nThe exponent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{24}\n$$\n\nshows that for every increase in $s=24$ seconds, the exponent increases by $1$.\n\nTherefore, the number of packets is expected to increase by $5\\%$ every ${24}$ seconds."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212879 :question/id #uuid "5adc477e-3e18-5c72-83c4-696d8427b979" :question/math-academy-id "e-19061" :question/problem "The function\n$P(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}$\nmodels the population of bacteria in a sealed container $t$ weeks after a chemical is introduced. According to the model, what percentage is the population predicted to decrease by every $2$ days? Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Explanation:\n\nWe are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nP(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the population $t$ weeks after the chemical is introduced. We want to rewrite this model in terms of days instead of weeks.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $7$ days in a week, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{d}{7}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $7$ (not multiply) because we want $d=7$ days to correspond to $t=1$ week.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $p(d)$ for the population as a function of time in days $d$ after the chemical is introduced:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(d) = P(\\frac{d}{7}) &= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4) \\cdot (d/7)} \\\\\n&= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{3d/28}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage decrease over $2$ days. Inspecting the model in terms of days $p(d)$, we see that the decay factor over $d$ days is\n$(0.95)^{3d/28}$.\n\nSo, the decay factor over $2$ days is\n\n$$\n(0.95)^{3\\cdot 2/28} = (0.95)^{3/14} \\approx 0.989\\,069\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to a decrease of approximately\n\n$$\n1 - 0.989\\,069 = 0.010\\,931 \\approx 1.09\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the population is predicted to decrease by $1.09\\%$ every $2$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212914 :question/id #uuid "dfe256e2-93e1-5c91-9df2-b197fd24ca51" :question/math-academy-id "e-19116" :question/problem "If $\\dfrac{8k + 1}{5} = \\dfrac{8k + 1}{9}$, then, find the value of $8k + 2$." :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8k + 1}{5} = \\dfrac{8k + 1}{9}\n$$\n\nThe numerators on both sides are the same, but the denominators are different. The only way this equation can be true is if the numerator is $0$.\n\nFirst, we set\n\n$$\n8k + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8k + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n8k &= -1 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\nk = -\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\ninto the expression $8k + 2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8k + 2 &= 8 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) + 2 \\\\\n&=-1 + 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212972 :question/id #uuid "97a91ee6-d302-5452-bd82-b9ee7a08df30" :question/math-academy-id "e-19807" :question/problem "The acceleration of a train is measured as $5.1$ meters per second squared. What is this rate in miles per minute squared, rounding to the nearest tenth?\n\n*Hint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} \\approx 1,609\\,\\textrm{m}$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe'll need two conversion factors: one to convert the distance unit ($\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$), and one to convert the squared time unit ($\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $5.1\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- There are $1,609$ meters in a mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{1,609}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}}$\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$60\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of square seconds in one square minute, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$60(\\frac{s}{min})^{2}|= 60^{2}\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = 3, 600\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.1 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.1 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  11.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the train is accelerating at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n11.4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea69871-07e3-5424-9424-9081802d7bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/((x - 3)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210609} {:answer/id #uuid "9e143b35-3d62-5d74-b8aa-e86db7f302b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10)/((x - 3)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210610} {:answer/id #uuid "c715d65d-d05a-58fc-8eb6-f1f393949dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/(2(x - 3)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210611} {:answer/id #uuid "e35c1470-287b-5a47-8e39-4fa827d3ed38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/(4(x - 3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210612} {:answer/id #uuid "31fd43b3-0097-59cd-8c56-c8a27d5ba23e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/(8(x - 3)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210613}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb17d30-3560-5ca1-be0e-86cc6b946e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210608}] :question/id #uuid "adec39fb-e684-59a9-8331-8dbfc5f84769" :question/math-academy-id "q-289001" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $((\\frac{10}{x - 3}))/(4x - 12)$, where $x \\ne 3$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3c1911c-084a-52ce-bcda-b6f86400f66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210627} {:answer