value "T(t) = 128 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216516} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb7cf2e-4063-5338-be97-2972ea767410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 194 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216517} {:answer/id #uuid "23cc23ac-9948-5249-bc71-0ed899803769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 194 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216518}] :answer-field/id #uuid "daec1266-a9dd-5bb0-8df2-1707a183033f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216513}] :question/id #uuid "f7bc92b8-fc52-59ba-a433-20edfd6be299" :question/math-academy-id "q-327458" :question/problem "In a data center, a warm heat sink was placed on a tray in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $66^{∘}F$. At $t = 0$ minutes, the heat sink’s temperature was $194^{∘}F$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $178^{∘}F$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $164^{∘}F$. The heat sink continued to cool over time.\n\nWhich of the following functions best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the heat sink $t$ minutes after it was placed on the tray?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e4329c-f59e-50af-82f6-bed8f41a1de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305499} {:answer/id #uuid "84b0163c-45e3-514d-a83c-ab39e8e00fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306415} {:answer/id #uuid "a11ad399-54da-5b14-9b47-556b3e072d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306663} {:answer/id #uuid "bc87a16a-0e2e-5b54-9e49-126ddd8e00ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306908} {:answer/id #uuid "d64d68b5-2e8c-527d-ae3e-aa8790d57073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a11ad399-54da-5b14-9b47-556b3e072d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306663} :answer-field/id #uuid "79eaab3d-a2a0-5b82-bfca-f63fc030ff15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a266b256-994a-5563-82c8-322702645919" :question/math-academy-id "q-334398" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2p \\\\ y &=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80000d4c-7739-5f64-a737-2ea473eeaf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309751} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef16fa-44ab-5bb3-afcb-42dbcf98c3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef16fa-44ab-5bb3-afcb-42dbcf98c3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310721} :answer-field/id #uuid "03fdb7f2-eca0-5406-840d-1205920d819e" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7612abb-a919-5c4a-a907-517d58064003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310350} {:answer/id #uuid "f474d878-a88b-5654-9c8f-20eea8ff9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f474d878-a88b-5654-9c8f-20eea8ff9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310692} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d7ec927-bf4e-51f2-bebb-45bc25fd6acd" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310997} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd839b9-b394-5da3-8b03-323a1dbf1f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310382} {:answer/id #uuid "f47380b8-f259-5e0e-89ca-d299b5db0b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd839b9-b394-5da3-8b03-323a1dbf1f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310382} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ddec07-26b4-5ab3-b1a9-61d404da5ff8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "312bf705-0322-512f-9e06-34cf1b93dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309119} {:answer/id #uuid "8cca350c-e9e2-5182-9c72-83b27c95af13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309859} {:answer/id #uuid "bab87077-43ce-5c19-a7e1-728cd9be1d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310263} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9d7e62-e936-5eeb-b268-3a1855d9ec01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310418} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c2cd7e-7dc6-594e-b28b-77490d3513fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "312bf705-0322-512f-9e06-34cf1b93dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309119} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c45c37-a023-53c3-ac8e-d4201d7ee14f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1b58dd-d9eb-5759-b200-6a40e1aaa8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309095} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8f3b34-13ee-52fb-a1af-ec369edc2187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309467} {:answer/id #uuid "9683dd3c-7513-5249-90cd-03ae51c967d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309953} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a58b64-5aee-5dcd-91f8-3e6c8600c520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310201} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3fb1f9-5e91-5a7a-bc06-3c46d8a954ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e1b58dd-d9eb-5759-b200-6a40e1aaa8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309095} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e56fcc-74b8-5f27-abf5-5b0786ac27bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1779cd6b-e209-53e3-8f4f-5bba29090ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308933} {:answer/id #uuid "33987127-5ef9-5c93-a731-5e687b762115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309141} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea2c75b-5361-5842-9114-04ecca88c736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309603} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ee95c9-1cd6-5871-bb8e-20252dbb75a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33987127-5ef9-5c93-a731-5e687b762115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309141} :answer-field/id #uuid "f65f0ce5-d1f3-5655-a171-39abea665684" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a8802d-3d4d-5564-8390-a80c9704bb8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337133" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\nx\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}(x\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220962 :question/id #uuid "f0d8a828-410f-5a1f-8830-4ab0a0f55319" :question/math-academy-id "e-19383" :question/problem "What is the volume, in cubic meters, of a sphere with a radius of $\\dfrac{9}{2}$ meters?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the radius of a sphere and asked to find its volume.\n\nThe formula for the volume of a sphere is\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius.\n\nLet's substitute\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}π(\\frac{9}{2})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}π \\cdot \\frac{729}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{243π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{243\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncubic meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221126 :question/id #uuid "c862ff73-43f1-5d16-a58d-27b7338bdf76" :question/math-academy-id "e-21104" :question/problem "Two similar toy packaging boxes, $U$ and $W$, are in the shape of right rectangular prisms. The surface area of box $U$ is $25~\\mathrm{in}^2$, and the surface area of box $W$ is $49~\\mathrm{in}^2$. The volume of box $W$ is $686~\\mathrm{in}^3$. What is the total volume, in cubic inches $(\\mathrm{in}^3)$, of both boxes?" :question/worked-solution "First, let’s introduce our notation:\n\n- Let $k$ be the scale factor of similarity between the two boxes.\n- Let $S_U = 25$ and $S_W = 49$ represent the surface areas of boxes $U$ and $W$, respectively.\n- Let $V_U$ and $V_W = 686$ represent the volumes of boxes $U$ and $W$, respectively.\n\nSince the ratio of surface areas equals the square of the scale factor, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{U}}{S_{W}}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the volume of similar solids is proportional to the cube of their scale factor. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{V_{U}}{V_{W}} &= (\\frac{5}{7})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{125}{343}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $V_U$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{U} &= \\frac{125}{343} \\cdot V_{W} \\\\\n&= \\frac{125}{343} \\cdot 686 \\\\\n&= 250\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{U} + V_{W} &= 250 + 686 \\\\\n&= 936in^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221154 :question/id #uuid "503c762b-0313-598a-a80c-c53d14ec250a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21318" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2y + e^x, \\qquad y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221160 :question/id #uuid "14669099-db40-5dd2-b4d5-4db016374643" :question/math-academy-id "e-21538" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x-y \\\\  & =-0-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{7}{8})=-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-1-(-\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{39}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221161 :question/id #uuid "c736760f-414e-5ab2-adfa-c09acce10d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21539" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x - 2y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14,$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221172 :question/id #uuid "15820b47-eda7-55a7-9027-3a41831c71dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21661" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x^2 - y, \\qquad y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\qquad y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac12, \\qquad y = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac12 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\frac{1}{3}+2\\cdot {1}^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\ny(1)\\approx\\dfrac{14}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221174 :question/id #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" :question/math-academy-id "e-21665" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](images/82/82b6d431638e3abb1c1aebf818f3fbe9f2eb92c7232a3b83c93dc6cb4e563c91.svg)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221176 :question/id #uuid "62004d73-b9ca-5133-b016-e2694a55211c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21668" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx^2y'' + 2y' = 4x^2, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\qquad y'(1) = -1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1) = y'(1) = -1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at $x=\\dfrac32,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32$\nand\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221195 :question/id #uuid "fac2d0c3-b5e2-5683-ab0a-74fc8d8bf136" :question/math-academy-id "e-21834" :question/problem "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t \\cos 5t\\}.\n$$\n\n \nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{\\cos \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = \\cos 5t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega = 5,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+25},\n\\qquad s > 0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}+25)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}+25)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}+25)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+25}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-25}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221198 :question/id #uuid "823fc00b-48af-5e79-9dcc-5e519e81cd4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21837" :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+4$ for $x\\in[-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_3.$" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-5x+4$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2} (-5x+4)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\cdot (-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }[(-5(2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-(-5(-2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi )]+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{20}{9{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221199 :question/id #uuid "07ff9076-2942-574e-98ae-fdd80d69109f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21838" :question/problem "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^2 \\sinh{5t}\\}.\n$$\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{\\sinh \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2-\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > \\omega.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2 f(t) \\} = F''(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = \\sinh 5t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega = 5,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{5}{s^2-25},\n\\qquad s > 5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5({s}^{2}-25)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-5({s}^{2}-25)^{-2}\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-\\frac{10s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{10s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-25)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-25)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}-25)}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}-25-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{-3{s}^{2}-25}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30{s}^{2}+250}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find $F''(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5t}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{30{s}^{2}+250}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}},\\,s>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221200 :question/id #uuid "669126b0-2995-549c-9a80-888bccd3acc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21845" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[-5,5).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/64/64e7a907f9d039e0b8a9ff71ba4bab18a9325a8322552cda69391e239bf3339f.svg)\nSince $f$ is ![](images/9c/9c95f62579d17c626e85bd76ba0028b378e8a0a3c2b9879d8e596bd9773eea84.svg)\nand  ![](images/90/90d690278324e4611fb59fa60aae82457d5ee10e595057bdd9d9f0e8a6a9db93.svg)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/68/689e50221843f953a679c91852b2cc11b4b89cb8aa86613be59fcec65ac75c43.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{107}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221202 :question/id #uuid "77c71e9d-c2a4-5a2c-9c67-a90e8913ba13" :question/math-academy-id "e-21856" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/ca/cad855e780f39df95e18c8c89e1994a1f60e5609bae77be32d779c2a37dfb7c5.svg)\n Since $f$ is \n![](images/ec/ec188620716be3a7ba759589ec331eb4a2b0b8c522b45382f535889d1c980760.svg)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/62/627f5788ec8cf06c5cf32245f8128edba2de2d5941f1e4ffcdc61645ab97c27d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221203 :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0} &=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n} &=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05919e85-7d81-5181-ad98-a99eee4791a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302494} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea4697c-e006-5da2-8ed2-c3f8a7041db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302745} {:answer/id #uuid "34031a95-96b3-5498-a917-6d9a4f18502d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302780} {:answer/id #uuid "3eeefefc-5dae-5569-9967-19169382fb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302850} {:answer/id #uuid "d4af1085-1ddb-541f-ba6a-808e18e66659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05919e85-7d81-5181-ad98-a99eee4791a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302494} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb67c0a5-88e2-53a9-ae43-6c89a0a1d7ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1a505b5-5206-5aba-aeb7-87c9ff569a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-329310" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3+x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3+x-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =3+0-2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3+1-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{43}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{31}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{3})+8\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{83}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{83}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+(-\\frac{83}{36}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{13}{36}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(1)≈\\frac{13}{36}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4284649e-a8db-5346-a5ea-477f8ac7dd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4284649e-a8db-5346-a5ea-477f8ac7dd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302875} :answer-field/id #uuid "281b19ea-c657-5905-91d4-a5b5fc025fbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d54a33-a989-54cf-8dd4-03f68cd2e0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d54a33-a989-54cf-8dd4-03f68cd2e0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302922} :answer-field/id #uuid "d206026e-2436-5f73-8e49-cd25b7d1569c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38da3904-9fde-586f-8e6e-003c3a863a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{137}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38da3904-9fde-586f-8e6e-003c3a863a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{137}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302807} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63afe3c-a674-5b4b-b78d-cee502bce68a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74629dbe-868c-5d08-adfe-776d31c19676" :question/math-academy-id "q-329474" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y+6,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-3y+6 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-3\\cdot \\frac{27}{16}+6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{29}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{16}+\\frac{29}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{137}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ | $\\frac{137}{64}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe940d5d-5e4a-52c4-9bba-b0a542545be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe940d5d-5e4a-52c4-9bba-b0a542545be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307502} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e30f8c-efcb-5fd0-9590-05971f5ff166" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "646fef1b-fc70-5ec7-b572-5dd3fd734b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646fef1b-fc70-5ec7-b572-5dd3fd734b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306120} :answer-field/id #uuid "777ff432-698f-5cc8-87bb-1e91698a452e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "585a6b31-349e-5195-b6c5-30ce9e2bf96b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332989" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′}=2x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{4})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})=z(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26719a06-d03a-5350-93e6-c3250a735c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26719a06-d03a-5350-93e6-c3250a735c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305509} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e406c0-5264-56ff-b2b4-b02995e18d9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307832} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af4235c-4ff1-57de-827a-7af2f133d0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af4235c-4ff1-57de-827a-7af2f133d0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305565} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57e953d-7ce4-5243-b6c5-6839e6c8d894" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b249fb96-97ce-504b-b915-d6a5be59728f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b249fb96-97ce-504b-b915-d6a5be59728f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306827} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2acf9df-fd9e-5f3b-9dd2-ceac9f3604bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bd5c73e-14a0-5ec3-b975-8bd0d7a8fc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333066" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-1,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4})-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f05a57c5-daa4-5016-9fee-e90e47d3457c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220470} {:answer-field/id #uuid "fc6cf40b-3a43-5185-9bf6-fbd30cf14077" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220471} {:answer-field/id #uuid "558a0cdf-bff7-5839-9bf4-04df86bfe380" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220472}] :question/id #uuid "48f26100-1b9f-55c4-92a3-aa14f6bb311e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333555" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 4y = 3x, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) =-1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | $z^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2}) \\approx$\n{{field-3}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e592a3c-a447-5305-88fd-b44acf542e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220522} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9ae693-9549-59ea-820f-b230558cdfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220523} {:answer/id #uuid "c74fdbc0-e3d8-5ce5-972e-ef4722902fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220524} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7640a2-1a0b-5579-9334-a3b8db93cf95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220525} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3721a7-62a3-5b80-ad44-3c967b9b0f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220526}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5f80cb-06e4-5abe-8aab-3b1a0ea1975a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220521} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7893789-fbde-532e-8614-351e660f4040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374737} {:answer/id #uuid "bb81a5a3-ae2a-5a83-8f23-621a2e29e89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374738} {:answer/id #uuid "c64f8c89-f90a-5695-92ef-f894fcd4704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374739} {:answer/id #uuid "fb30dce0-e1af-5379-8d1b-af83ba62b18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374740} {:answer/id #uuid "7047d73f-bcaa-5ae8-970a-c015cdc10020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb30dce0-e1af-5379-8d1b-af83ba62b18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374740} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db3b47f-593d-5348-aa66-c376288844f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b365aa8d-ee89-53d2-9ac1-9b5758d36ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333679" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-y-8,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(2)≈-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =4{x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2-8 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{1}{4})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-y-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-(-\\frac{1}{4})-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-(-6))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{27}{8}=\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-\\frac{25}{8}-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-(-6))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{99}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{33}{32}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{33}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{33}{32}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{25}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{25}{32}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30706d91-e588-580c-ba78-08bcbf1f612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312366} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1f9b18-6f3f-5382-8b2c-cd89263af4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313081} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdaa246-2de1-5473-9b10-1196f03686e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313233} {:answer/id #uuid "e2cfb239-e274-504e-85c6-cc642d08b9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b1f9b18-6f3f-5382-8b2c-cd89263af4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313081} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaeaa0a7-049d-58c5-acba-d20f0b0497b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1db9f63-1290-512d-8842-531c9d1381d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339635" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈2.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈2.67.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1530fae3-2cf9-55cb-820c-dcfe64d44d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312123} {:answer/id #uuid "e4eec080-2ace-54d6-ba9b-b2d4812913f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313783} {:answer/id #uuid "e6270282-29da-5702-8057-2815a0fee32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4eec080-2ace-54d6-ba9b-b2d4812913f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313783} :answer-field/id #uuid "26327356-123c-5904-8a2d-6b0b25c24fef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe71bcb-46dd-51fa-8515-349ead9b3553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312216} {:answer/id #uuid "925a9f4f-cb46-50b5-b3bd-9102c799b96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313151} {:answer/id #uuid "b94ec3d2-84b8-5b72-a2d3-15bb021a3549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe71bcb-46dd-51fa-8515-349ead9b3553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312216} :answer-field/id #uuid "f759d688-4636-5621-b022-b2a805cdd56e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20432103-aae2-58eb-a669-f0d5cfc82ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339642" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0.6+1)}{5} \\\\  & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈0.60,\\,y(1)≈0.64.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b63046b-e2c7-5f83-8cc7-dd28251d333d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220753} {:answer/id #uuid "ad76fd24-8271-5d02-9e20-84c4501894f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220754} {:answer/id #uuid "278389eb-4ec6-503e-83b9-1a7aa9d81ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220755} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6c8c05-5d7a-5a72-a6f0-3bd41844dd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220756}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e4ec8f1-3e4d-5003-b569-f91516ed128f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220752}] :question/id #uuid "6eddd966-6f37-550f-a4f4-75f9781285b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339688" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = y(x^{4} + 1), y(0) = 3$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$y(\\frac{1}{2}) =$ {{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3536e38d-e225-53b8-9e9e-d97755983fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312403} {:answer/id #uuid "448a7a91-8286-5a01-a0f4-da8365ab39f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312527} {:answer/id #uuid "88fb9f41-9283-5a9d-9344-b746d81a2a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3536e38d-e225-53b8-9e9e-d97755983fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312403} :answer-field/id #uuid "248de7b2-ae84-5a17-a664-fabf77196ef3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a74779-82b2-5d14-9f4c-8e2bf01bd107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312027} {:answer/id #uuid "cf07a8ff-a178-59e8-b28a-659169974d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313594} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59cecc-653c-558d-978f-cf97c789b39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a74779-82b2-5d14-9f4c-8e2bf01bd107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312027} :answer-field/id #uuid "5293d2ed-b608-52cb-a5d8-a25e5ab5e15f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc5b86b1-8128-5d8b-a239-fcd531c52211" :question/math-academy-id "q-339722" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+{e}^{1/2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈3.898\\,721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y≈3.898\\,721.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈2(3.898\\,721)+{e}^{1}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ≈11.515\\,724\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, rounded to $2$ decimal places, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.90,\\,y(1)≈11.52.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056e4360-8236-5b03-9584-107912df8d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312011} {:answer/id #uuid "0d22f379-da60-50ab-bc76-62d1c65ad841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312069} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c40ad8-7270-52bb-9ca0-350e4f4208d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c40ad8-7270-52bb-9ca0-350e4f4208d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313349} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fab8ed3-33d3-5459-8db6-52dd29b67c27" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73388ba1-c7d9-548f-9625-ed4589f02bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312885} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f84507-0eac-53aa-8b13-9fef473c2b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313359} {:answer/id #uuid "f7240911-1584-56a5-a89d-170795cd3a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7240911-1584-56a5-a89d-170795cd3a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313950} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ab139e3-35ee-5146-b0cd-7fff36c68f47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05d2dcb4-6d58-5ba1-932c-6420aacb3731" :question/math-academy-id "q-339771" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+4y,\\,y(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(0)$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x+4y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 0+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =-10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈3.00,\\,y(1)≈-10.50.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c2e2b4-053f-5a67-8bc0-6f6fddc281a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312101} {:answer/id #uuid "30e450d1-8593-5124-9761-5e2a6a6b3cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312370} {:answer/id #uuid "c4694c8d-b63a-524f-b372-7f98a2d76882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4694c8d-b63a-524f-b372-7f98a2d76882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313517} :answer-field/id #uuid "c98303c1-37c4-526f-9d5b-461b3ddd51d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09889eb4-4b65-5589-a105-ce8a45a52d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312045} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fa8bc3-13da-5e18-875c-756e4a63773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313284} {:answer/id #uuid "b4594e7e-e78e-569f-9338-0997cd3957e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6fa8bc3-13da-5e18-875c-756e4a63773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313284} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0d0024-a9b9-59c9-af6e-123e2cb677c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f888fbe-20c0-5862-9487-c8905d7f153c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339772" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & ≈1.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈1.14.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499e08a2-9189-5bb0-9f9f-0a461fa706bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{F}^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312568} {:answer/id #uuid "839c4814-adeb-5f60-a813-277e6f1f9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}{F}^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313034} {:answer/id #uuid "87b97734-7174-5994-9dc0-ae353742152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{F}^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313058} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd2b7e8-8417-5270-9980-b2448d4dd552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{s}^{2}{F}^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313719} {:answer/id #uuid "fc985d52-79e1-5eb9-9654-2b2c211e77e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{s}^{2}{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499e08a2-9189-5bb0-9f9f-0a461fa706bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{F}^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312568} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba73807-8cac-5e10-9d3f-0f6218876680" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fe13db-4ae3-5189-9875-3c295da57ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{